poslib's zettelkasten

One zettle for each idea needed to understand poslib and its protocols. The
zettles are Org files, indexed by org-roam and linked to each other. Every
zettle follows the template and the rules below. A zettle that breaks a rule
is corrected or removed.

Reading it in Emacs

The zettelkasten stands alone. Point org-roam at this directory:

    (setq org-roam-directory "/path/to/poslib/zettles/")
    (org-roam-db-sync)

M-x org-roam-node-find then finds a zettle by its title, a link opens the
zettle it names, and M-x org-roam-buffer-toggle shows the zettles that
link to the one in view. Emacs then reads this zettelkasten and no other,
until org-roam-directory is set back.

The template

    :PROPERTIES:
    :ID:       <new UUID>
    :END:
    #+TITLE: <the idea, as a noun or short noun phrase>

    <The idea> is <what it is in poslib, in one plain sentence>. Or, for
    a rule: <The rule> states that <what it requires>.

    <One paragraph that says how the idea relates to other ideas in the
    zettelkasten. Each other idea's name is a
    link: [[id:<UUID>][name of the other zettle]]. Where the idea or its
    name comes from outside poslib, a footnote says so.[fn:1]>

    [fn:1] <Who and what it comes from>: [[https://example.org/][title of the web page]].

The rules

A zettle

- A zettle holds one idea. When a zettle comes to hold two, it is split into
  two zettles that link to each other.
- A zettle is the definition, then the paragraph, then any footnotes. It has
  no headings, lists, tables or further paragraphs.
- A zettle is written in the present tense and holds no status, dates or
  history.

The words

- Plain English throughout: short sentences and everyday words.
- Where a thing has a technical name an expert knows, such as IPFS, CID,
  Merkle tree or HAMT, the zettle uses that name, in a plain sentence, with
  a footnote that links to it.
- An id is called an id.

The first sentence

- The first sentence defines the idea. It begins with the idea's name.
- It is written in plain language. A reader who has not read poslib's
  specifications understands it at first reading.
- It says what the idea is in poslib. It is not a dictionary definition:
  "A ledger is the ordered record of every seal made into one archive",
  not "A ledger is a book of accounts".
- It is one sentence. A definition that needs a second sentence is
  rewritten.
- It makes one claim. It has no list of parts or qualities joined by
  commas and "and"; what else is true of the idea goes in the paragraph.

The paragraph

- One paragraph follows the definition. It explains how the idea relates
  to other ideas in the zettelkasten.
- The paragraph is full of links. Every idea it names that has a zettle is
  an id: link to that zettle, on the idea's name. A paragraph with few
  links is rewritten to name the ideas it relies on.
- Each relation is stated with an exact verb: contains, is made by,
  identifies, replaces, is checked against. "Is related to" and "see
  also" say nothing.
- Where two zettles are related, each says so from its own side.
- The paragraph does not repeat the specification. It says enough that
  the specification can then be read.

Footnotes

- Footnotes are optional. A footnote says where an idea or its name
  comes from outside poslib: the person, the work, and links to pages on
  the web, such as Wikipedia or the original text.
- Links to the web appear in footnotes only. The definition and the
  paragraph link to zettles of the zettelkasten only.

The file

- A zettle is one file directly in this directory, named for its title in
  lower case with hyphens: recorded-root.org.
- Its first line opens a property drawer that holds its Org ID. The ID
  never changes; the title and the file name may.
- The title is the idea's name as the first sentence uses it.
